NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"metadata-rebuild-after-a-nested-transaction","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Metadata rebuild after a nested transaction"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Base revision: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"65992aacd"},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"},{"type":"text","value":"). Found while triaging state mutation round 4."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"law","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Law"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Row metadata in one sync transaction follows last write wins, by the rules in\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"collection-row-metadata-composition-oracle.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":". The rules apply to the\nwrite that applies. When the Collection reclassifies an insert, metadata\nfollows the new classification."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"the-failure","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The failure"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A transaction begun inside an open transaction can commit first. The open\ntransaction then applies after it, and the Collection rebuilds the open\ntransaction against the new projection. The rebuild reclassifies each insert:\nan insert of a present key with an equal value is a re-insert, and a re-insert\nof an absent key is an insert."}]},{"type":"code","value":"sync.begin() // T1, row 1 is present\nsync.metadata.row.set(1, { m: `w0` })\nsync.write({ type: `insert`, value: { id: 1, v: 0 } }) // equal re-insert\nsync.begin() // T2\nsync.write({ type: `delete`, key: 1 })\nsync.commit() // T2 applies first, so T1's insert is now an insert\nsync.commit() // T1\nsync.metadata.row.get(1) // main: { m: `w0` }; expected: undefined","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The reverse direction also fails. From an absent row, T1 sets metadata and\ninserts the row. T2 inserts the same row with metadata and commits first.\nT1's insert becomes a re-insert, which keeps the set value. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"},{"type":"text","value":" reads\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cause."}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rebuildAutomaticRowMetadataWrites"},{"type":"text","value":" skipped every key with an\nexplicit write. It kept the write that was current when T1 wrote, which no\nlonger follows from T1's operations."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Repair."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Each explicit write records how many operations came before it.\nThe rebuild clears the key's writes, restores the last explicit write, and\nreplays the automatic write of each later operation. Hydrated metadata\nrecords a position after every hydrated row, so it still holds."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"why-the-oracles-missed-it","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Why the oracles missed it"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The round 3 record called RB2, a mutant that keeps stale automatic writes in\nthe rebuild, equivalent within legal histories. The follow-up record then said\nthat #2030 made it unreachable, because only the open last transaction can be\ncanceled. Both records missed nested transactions. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"invalidationError"},{"type":"text","value":"\ncomment on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PendingSyncedTransaction"},{"type":"text","value":" names the history: a later transaction\nbegun inside an open one commits first. The metadata oracle's rebuilt lane\nchanges the projection only through an earlier transaction, which cannot\nreclassify an insert that is still open."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"witness","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Witness"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A nested lane in the metadata composition oracle opens a transaction, T1. T1\nwrites up to three of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"set"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"unset"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"truncate"},{"type":"text","value":", and an insert, update, or\ndelete of one fixed row value, each with or without metadata. Then a nested\nwrite applies before T1 commits:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"From a present row, a nested transaction deletes the row."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"From an absent row, a nested transaction inserts the same row with its own\nmetadata."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"From an absent row, a hydration seed inserts the same row with its own\nmetadata. The Collection places a seed ahead of the first open transaction."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A nested transaction can also call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"metadata.row.set"},{"type":"text","value":" after its row write. A\nhistory is legal when T1's writes are legal both where T1 writes them and\nwhere they apply."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The model reclassifies T1's inserts by the row's presence where they apply,\nand folds T1's writes over the value that the nested write leaves. It reads no\nproduction state. The lane runs 1,925 histories: 275 for each of seven\nvariants."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Revision"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Nested lane (of 1,925)"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"35 fail: all three nested writes, with and without a nested set"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"this fix"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"pass"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"explicit write always wins (M1)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"279 fail; the rebuilt lane also fails"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"keep stale automatic writes (M2, RB2)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"21 fail"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"explicit position recorded as 0 (M4)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"267 fail; the rebuilt lane also fails"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"hydrated metadata recorded at position 0"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"7 fail, all hydration seeds"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"explicit write recorded in the first open transaction"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"818 fail, all with a nested set"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"truncate keeps explicit positions"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"63 fail; the immediate, held, and rebuilt lanes also fail"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The hydration mutant also fails "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DbClient > hydrates pending collection rows when the collection materializes"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"review-outcomes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Review outcomes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A medium code review found no correctness bug and seven items. A simplifier\npass found one item, the same as item 5."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"explicitRowMetadataWrites"},{"type":"text","value":" was optional, though every constructor supplies\nit. Fixed: the field is required, and two test fixtures now supply it."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"metadata.row.set"},{"type":"text","value":" still writes to a transaction that a replay has\ninvalidated, while "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"write"},{"type":"text","value":" ignores it. This predates the fix, and the commit\ndiscards the transaction. Open: it belongs with round 4's SY8 survivor,\nwrites after invalidation, in the round 4 gaps change."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hydration could carry metadata on its operations instead of a parallel\nmap. Not adopted: an insert without metadata would then clear metadata\nthat a source kept for an absent key, which changes hydration."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An ordered log of metadata writes would avoid positions. Not adopted: a\ntransaction only appends operations, and a truncate clears both lists\ntogether."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hydration and the new helper spelled out "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PendingMetadataWrite"},{"type":"text","value":". Fixed."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The exported type was not formatted. Fixed."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The nested lane had no timeout, unlike the other lanes. Fixed."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"second-review","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Second review"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A second medium code review, of #2048 at "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"9e34d6c94"},{"type":"text","value":", found no correctness\nbug and seven items."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"metadata.row.set"},{"type":"text","value":" writes to an invalidated transaction. The same as item\n2 above. Open, with round 4's SY8."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"T1 never wrote an update, a delete, or a truncate. Fixed: the lane\ngenerates them where they are legal in both places."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No lane reached a hydration seed ahead of an open transaction. A probe\nshowed that "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"},{"type":"text","value":" reads "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"},{"type":"text","value":" where last write wins expects the\nexplicit set. Fixed: the seed is a nested write."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A nested transaction made no explicit metadata call, so a mutant that\nwrites to the first open transaction survived. Fixed: the nested set."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The rebuild rescans every operation of every queued transaction. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"},{"type":"text","value":"\nhas the same loop, and this change adds no rebuild. No change."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hydration builds its explicit map in a second pass. A one-loop version\nadded seven production lines to save one copy per chunk. Not adopted."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The fix adds production lines against the bug-fix budget. A position-only\nmap still needs the explicit value, so no smaller design was found."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"orc-outcomes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC outcomes"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The contract is the metadata oracle's opening prose. Its\nlimits now state that the rules follow the reclassified write."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-002: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reclassify"},{"type":"text","value":" derives each insert's kind from the row's\npresence. It does not call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"classifyProjectedInsert"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-003: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The lane's grammar, model step, and driver step each have\nprose beside them."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-004: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The lane is exhaustive within its bound. A count control\nchecks 1,925 histories, named witnesses cover each variant, and two\nexclusions check the legality rule. Every row write uses one value, so no\nreclassified insert is a duplicate."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-005: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The driver writes through a real Collection's sync API and\nreads "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"metadata.row.get"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-006: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" See the table above."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-007: not applicable."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The lane is exhaustive. It has no random\ncampaign."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-008: not applicable."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The model gains no state."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-009: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" \"Nested transaction\" means a sync transaction begun while\nan earlier one is open. It is the history that "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"invalidationError"},{"type":"text","value":" names."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-010: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The driver cleans up each Collection in a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"finally"},{"type":"text","value":"\nblock."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-011: not applicable."}]},{"type":"text","value":" No reviewer named a shared fault."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-012: met by this record."}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-013: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Each mutant in the table fails a distinct part of the\nlane. The hydration and first-open-transaction mutants fail only the seed\nand nested-set variants."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-014: not applicable."}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"limits","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Limits"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The lane covers one key. A nested truncate, a nested update, or a nested\nwrite to another key is not generated. A nested insert with a different value\nmakes the open transaction a duplicate. The sync reentrancy oracle owns that\ninvalidation."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"metadata-rebuild-after-a-nested-transaction","text":"Metadata rebuild after a nested transaction","level":1},{"id":"law","text":"Law","level":2},{"id":"the-failure","text":"The failure","level":2},{"id":"why-the-oracles-missed-it","text":"Why the oracles missed it","level":2},{"id":"witness","text":"Witness","level":2},{"id":"review-outcomes","text":"Review outcomes","level":2},{"id":"second-review","text":"Second review","level":2},{"id":"orc-outcomes","text":"ORC outcomes","level":2},{"id":"limits","text":"Limits","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"# Metadata rebuild after a nested transaction\n\nBase revision: `65992aacd` (`main`). Found while triaging state mutation round 4.\n\n## Law\n\nRow metadata in one sync transaction follows last write wins, by the rules in\n`collection-row-metadata-composition-oracle.test.ts`. The rules apply to the\nwrite that applies. When the Collection reclassifies an insert, metadata\nfollows the new classification.\n\n## The failure\n\nA transaction begun inside an open transaction can commit first. The open\ntransaction then applies after it, and the Collection rebuilds the open\ntransaction against the new projection. The rebuild reclassifies each insert:\nan insert of a present key with an equal value is a re-insert, and a re-insert\nof an absent key is an insert.\n\n```ts\nsync.begin() // T1, row 1 is present\nsync.metadata.row.set(1, { m: `w0` })\nsync.write({ type: `insert`, value: { id: 1, v: 0 } }) // equal re-insert\nsync.begin() // T2\nsync.write({ type: `delete`, key: 1 })\nsync.commit() // T2 applies first, so T1's insert is now an insert\nsync.commit() // T1\nsync.metadata.row.get(1) // main: { m: `w0` }; expected: undefined\n```\n\nThe reverse direction also fails. From an absent row, T1 sets metadata and\ninserts the row. T2 inserts the same row with metadata and commits first.\nT1's insert becomes a re-insert, which keeps the set value. `main` reads\n`undefined`.\n\n**Cause.** `rebuildAutomaticRowMetadataWrites` skipped every key with an\nexplicit write. It kept the write that was current when T1 wrote, which no\nlonger follows from T1's operations.\n\n**Repair.** Each explicit write records how many operations came before it.\nThe rebuild clears the key's writes, restores the last explicit write, and\nreplays the automatic write of each later operation. Hydrated metadata\nrecords a position after every hydrated row, so it still holds.\n\n## Why the oracles missed it\n\nThe round 3 record called RB2, a mutant that keeps stale automatic writes in\nthe rebuild, equivalent within legal histories. The follow-up record then said\nthat #2030 made it unreachable, because only the open last transaction can be\ncanceled. Both records missed nested transactions. The `invalidationError`\ncomment on `PendingSyncedTransaction` names the history: a later transaction\nbegun inside an open one commits first. The metadata oracle's rebuilt lane\nchanges the projection only through an earlier transaction, which cannot\nreclassify an insert that is still open.\n\n## Witness\n\nA nested lane in the metadata composition oracle opens a transaction, T1. T1\nwrites up to three of `set`, `unset`, `truncate`, and an insert, update, or\ndelete of one fixed row value, each with or without metadata. Then a nested\nwrite applies before T1 commits:\n\n- From a present row, a nested transaction deletes the row.\n- From an absent row, a nested transaction inserts the same row with its own\n  metadata.\n- From an absent row, a hydration seed inserts the same row with its own\n  metadata. The Collection places a seed ahead of the first open transaction.\n\nA nested transaction can also call `metadata.row.set` after its row write. A\nhistory is legal when T1's writes are legal both where T1 writes them and\nwhere they apply.\n\nThe model reclassifies T1's inserts by the row's presence where they apply,\nand folds T1's writes over the value that the nested write leaves. It reads no\nproduction state. The lane runs 1,925 histories: 275 for each of seven\nvariants.\n\n| Revision                                              | Nested lane (of 1,925)                                          |\n| ----------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `main`                                                | 35 fail: all three nested writes, with and without a nested set |\n| this fix                                              | pass                                                            |\n| explicit write always wins (M1)                       | 279 fail; the rebuilt lane also fails                           |\n| keep stale automatic writes (M2, RB2)                 | 21 fail                                                         |\n| explicit position recorded as 0 (M4)                  | 267 fail; the rebuilt lane also fails                           |\n| hydrated metadata recorded at position 0              | 7 fail, all hydration seeds                                     |\n| explicit write recorded in the first open transaction | 818 fail, all with a nested set                                 |\n| truncate keeps explicit positions                     | 63 fail; the immediate, held, and rebuilt lanes also fail       |\n\nThe hydration mutant also fails `DbClient > hydrates pending collection rows\nwhen the collection materializes`.\n\n## Review outcomes\n\nA medium code review found no correctness bug and seven items. A simplifier\npass found one item, the same as item 5.\n\n1. `explicitRowMetadataWrites` was optional, though every constructor supplies\n   it. Fixed: the field is required, and two test fixtures now supply it.\n2. `metadata.row.set` still writes to a transaction that a replay has\n   invalidated, while `write` ignores it. This predates the fix, and the commit\n   discards the transaction. Open: it belongs with round 4's SY8 survivor,\n   writes after invalidation, in the round 4 gaps change.\n3. Hydration could carry metadata on its operations instead of a parallel\n   map. Not adopted: an insert without metadata would then clear metadata\n   that a source kept for an absent key, which changes hydration.\n4. An ordered log of metadata writes would avoid positions. Not adopted: a\n   transaction only appends operations, and a truncate clears both lists\n   together.\n5. Hydration and the new helper spelled out `PendingMetadataWrite`. Fixed.\n6. The exported type was not formatted. Fixed.\n7. The nested lane had no timeout, unlike the other lanes. Fixed.\n\n## Second review\n\nA second medium code review, of #2048 at `9e34d6c94`, found no correctness\nbug and seven items.\n\n1. `metadata.row.set` writes to an invalidated transaction. The same as item\n   2 above. Open, with round 4's SY8.\n2. T1 never wrote an update, a delete, or a truncate. Fixed: the lane\n   generates them where they are legal in both places.\n3. No lane reached a hydration seed ahead of an open transaction. A probe\n   showed that `main` reads `undefined` where last write wins expects the\n   explicit set. Fixed: the seed is a nested write.\n4. A nested transaction made no explicit metadata call, so a mutant that\n   writes to the first open transaction survived. Fixed: the nested set.\n5. The rebuild rescans every operation of every queued transaction. `main`\n   has the same loop, and this change adds no rebuild. No change.\n6. Hydration builds its explicit map in a second pass. A one-loop version\n   added seven production lines to save one copy per chunk. Not adopted.\n7. The fix adds production lines against the bug-fix budget. A position-only\n   map still needs the explicit value, so no smaller design was found.\n\n## ORC outcomes\n\n- **ORC-001: met.** The contract is the metadata oracle's opening prose. Its\n  limits now state that the rules follow the reclassified write.\n- **ORC-002: met.** `reclassify` derives each insert's kind from the row's\n  presence. It does not call `classifyProjectedInsert`.\n- **ORC-003: met.** The lane's grammar, model step, and driver step each have\n  prose beside them.\n- **ORC-004: met.** The lane is exhaustive within its bound. A count control\n  checks 1,925 histories, named witnesses cover each variant, and two\n  exclusions check the legality rule. Every row write uses one value, so no\n  reclassified insert is a duplicate.\n- **ORC-005: met.** The driver writes through a real Collection's sync API and\n  reads `metadata.row.get`.\n- **ORC-006: met.** See the table above.\n- **ORC-007: not applicable.** The lane is exhaustive. It has no random\n  campaign.\n- **ORC-008: not applicable.** The model gains no state.\n- **ORC-009: met.** \"Nested transaction\" means a sync transaction begun while\n  an earlier one is open. It is the history that `invalidationError` names.\n- **ORC-010: met.** The driver cleans up each Collection in a `finally`\n  block.\n- **ORC-011: not applicable.** No reviewer named a shared fault.\n- **ORC-012: met by this record.**\n- **ORC-013: met.** Each mutant in the table fails a distinct part of the\n  lane. The hydration and first-open-transaction mutants fail only the seed\n  and nested-set variants.\n- **ORC-014: not applicable.**\n\n## Limits\n\nThe lane covers one key. A nested truncate, a nested update, or a nested\nwrite to another key is not generated. A nested insert with a different value\nmakes the open transaction a duplicate. The sync reentrancy oracle owns that\ninvalidation.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"# Metadata rebuild after a nested transaction\n\nBase revision: `65992aacd` (`main`). Found while triaging state mutation round 4.\n\n## Law\n\nRow metadata in one sync transaction follows last write wins, by the rules in\n`collection-row-metadata-composition-oracle.test.ts`. The rules apply to the\nwrite that applies. When the Collection reclassifies an insert, metadata\nfollows the new classification.\n\n## The failure\n\nA transaction begun inside an open transaction can commit first. The open\ntransaction then applies after it, and the Collection rebuilds the open\ntransaction against the new projection. The rebuild reclassifies each insert:\nan insert of a present key with an equal value is a re-insert, and a re-insert\nof an absent key is an insert.\n\n```ts\nsync.begin() // T1, row 1 is present\nsync.metadata.row.set(1, { m: `w0` })\nsync.write({ type: `insert`, value: { id: 1, v: 0 } }) // equal re-insert\nsync.begin() // T2\nsync.write({ type: `delete`, key: 1 })\nsync.commit() // T2 applies first, so T1's insert is now an insert\nsync.commit() // T1\nsync.metadata.row.get(1) // main: { m: `w0` }; expected: undefined\n```\n\nThe reverse direction also fails. From an absent row, T1 sets metadata and\ninserts the row. T2 inserts the same row with metadata and commits first.\nT1's insert becomes a re-insert, which keeps the set value. `main` reads\n`undefined`.\n\n**Cause.** `rebuildAutomaticRowMetadataWrites` skipped every key with an\nexplicit write. It kept the write that was current when T1 wrote, which no\nlonger follows from T1's operations.\n\n**Repair.** Each explicit write records how many operations came before it.\nThe rebuild clears the key's writes, restores the last explicit write, and\nreplays the automatic write of each later operation. Hydrated metadata\nrecords a position after every hydrated row, so it still holds.\n\n## Why the oracles missed it\n\nThe round 3 record called RB2, a mutant that keeps stale automatic writes in\nthe rebuild, equivalent within legal histories. The follow-up record then said\nthat #2030 made it unreachable, because only the open last transaction can be\ncanceled. Both records missed nested transactions. The `invalidationError`\ncomment on `PendingSyncedTransaction` names the history: a later transaction\nbegun inside an open one commits first. The metadata oracle's rebuilt lane\nchanges the projection only through an earlier transaction, which cannot\nreclassify an insert that is still open.\n\n## Witness\n\nA nested lane in the metadata composition oracle opens a transaction, T1. T1\nwrites up to three of `set`, `unset`, `truncate`, and an insert, update, or\ndelete of one fixed row value, each with or without metadata. Then a nested\nwrite applies before T1 commits:\n\n- From a present row, a nested transaction deletes the row.\n- From an absent row, a nested transaction inserts the same row with its own\n  metadata.\n- From an absent row, a hydration seed inserts the same row with its own\n  metadata. The Collection places a seed ahead of the first open transaction.\n\nA nested transaction can also call `metadata.row.set` after its row write. A\nhistory is legal when T1's writes are legal both where T1 writes them and\nwhere they apply.\n\nThe model reclassifies T1's inserts by the row's presence where they apply,\nand folds T1's writes over the value that the nested write leaves. It reads no\nproduction state. The lane runs 1,925 histories: 275 for each of seven\nvariants.\n\n| Revision                                              | Nested lane (of 1,925)                                          |\n| ----------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `main`                                                | 35 fail: all three nested writes, with and without a nested set |\n| this fix                                              | pass                                                            |\n| explicit write always wins (M1)                       | 279 fail; the rebuilt lane also fails                           |\n| keep stale automatic writes (M2, RB2)                 | 21 fail                                                         |\n| explicit position recorded as 0 (M4)                  | 267 fail; the rebuilt lane also fails                           |\n| hydrated metadata recorded at position 0              | 7 fail, all hydration seeds                                     |\n| explicit write recorded in the first open transaction | 818 fail, all with a nested set                                 |\n| truncate keeps explicit positions                     | 63 fail; the immediate, held, and rebuilt lanes also fail       |\n\nThe hydration mutant also fails `DbClient > hydrates pending collection rows\nwhen the collection materializes`.\n\n## Review outcomes\n\nA medium code review found no correctness bug and seven items. A simplifier\npass found one item, the same as item 5.\n\n1. `explicitRowMetadataWrites` was optional, though every constructor supplies\n   it. Fixed: the field is required, and two test fixtures now supply it.\n2. `metadata.row.set` still writes to a transaction that a replay has\n   invalidated, while `write` ignores it. This predates the fix, and the commit\n   discards the transaction. Open: it belongs with round 4's SY8 survivor,\n   writes after invalidation, in the round 4 gaps change.\n3. Hydration could carry metadata on its operations instead of a parallel\n   map. Not adopted: an insert without metadata would then clear metadata\n   that a source kept for an absent key, which changes hydration.\n4. An ordered log of metadata writes would avoid positions. Not adopted: a\n   transaction only appends operations, and a truncate clears both lists\n   together.\n5. Hydration and the new helper spelled out `PendingMetadataWrite`. Fixed.\n6. The exported type was not formatted. Fixed.\n7. The nested lane had no timeout, unlike the other lanes. Fixed.\n\n## Second review\n\nA second medium code review, of #2048 at `9e34d6c94`, found no correctness\nbug and seven items.\n\n1. `metadata.row.set` writes to an invalidated transaction. The same as item\n   2 above. Open, with round 4's SY8.\n2. T1 never wrote an update, a delete, or a truncate. Fixed: the lane\n   generates them where they are legal in both places.\n3. No lane reached a hydration seed ahead of an open transaction. A probe\n   showed that `main` reads `undefined` where last write wins expects the\n   explicit set. Fixed: the seed is a nested write.\n4. A nested transaction made no explicit metadata call, so a mutant that\n   writes to the first open transaction survived. Fixed: the nested set.\n5. The rebuild rescans every operation of every queued transaction. `main`\n   has the same loop, and this change adds no rebuild. No change.\n6. Hydration builds its explicit map in a second pass. A one-loop version\n   added seven production lines to save one copy per chunk. Not adopted.\n7. The fix adds production lines against the bug-fix budget. A position-only\n   map still needs the explicit value, so no smaller design was found.\n\n## ORC outcomes\n\n- **ORC-001: met.** The contract is the metadata oracle's opening prose. Its\n  limits now state that the rules follow the reclassified write.\n- **ORC-002: met.** `reclassify` derives each insert's kind from the row's\n  presence. It does not call `classifyProjectedInsert`.\n- **ORC-003: met.** The lane's grammar, model step, and driver step each have\n  prose beside them.\n- **ORC-004: met.** The lane is exhaustive within its bound. A count control\n  checks 1,925 histories, named witnesses cover each variant, and two\n  exclusions check the legality rule. Every row write uses one value, so no\n  reclassified insert is a duplicate.\n- **ORC-005: met.** The driver writes through a real Collection's sync API and\n  reads `metadata.row.get`.\n- **ORC-006: met.** See the table above.\n- **ORC-007: not applicable.** The lane is exhaustive. It has no random\n  campaign.\n- **ORC-008: not applicable.** The model gains no state.\n- **ORC-009: met.** \"Nested transaction\" means a sync transaction begun while\n  an earlier one is open. It is the history that `invalidationError` names.\n- **ORC-010: met.** The driver cleans up each Collection in a `finally`\n  block.\n- **ORC-011: not applicable.** No reviewer named a shared fault.\n- **ORC-012: met by this record.**\n- **ORC-013: met.** Each mutant in the table fails a distinct part of the\n  lane. The hydration and first-open-transaction mutants fail only the seed\n  and nested-set variants.\n- **ORC-014: not applicable.**\n\n## Limits\n\nThe lane covers one key. A nested truncate, a nested update, or a nested\nwrite to another key is not generated. A nested insert with a different value\nmakes the open transaction a duplicate. The sync reentrancy oracle owns that\ninvalidation.\n"}
